[agent:bash] databricks jobs --help | head -30
[agent:result] command: databricks jobs --help | head -30
[agent:result] stdout: The Jobs API allows you to create, edit, and delete jobs.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   You can use a Databricks job to run a data processing or data analysis task in
[agent:result]   a Databricks cluster with scalable resources. Your job can consist of a single
[agent:result]   task or can be a large, multi-task workflow with complex dependencies.
[agent:result]   Databricks manages the task orchestration, cluster management, monitoring, and
[agent:result]   error reporting for all of your jobs. You can run your jobs immediately or
[agent:result]   periodically through an easy-to-use scheduling system. You can implement job
[agent:result]   tasks using notebooks, JARS, Spark Declarative Pipelines, or Python, Scala,
[agent:result]   Spark submit, and Java applications.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   You should never hard code secrets or store them in plain text. Use the
[agent:result]   [Secrets CLI] to manage secrets in the [Databricks CLI]. Use the [Secrets
[agent:result]   utility] to reference secrets in notebooks and jobs.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   [Databricks CLI]: https://docs.databricks.com/dev-tools/cli/index.html
[agent:result]   [Secrets CLI]: https://docs.databricks.com/dev-tools/cli/secrets-cli.html
[agent:result]   [Secrets utility]: https://docs.databricks.com/dev-tools/databricks-utils.html#dbutils-secrets
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Usage:
[agent:result]   databricks jobs [flags]
[agent:result]   databricks jobs [command]
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Main Commands
[agent:result]   cancel-all-runs       Cancel all runs of a job.
[agent:result]   cancel-run            Cancel a run.
[agent:result]   create                Create a new job.
[agent:result]   delete                Delete a job.
[agent:result]   delete-run            Delete a job run.
[agent:result]   export-run            Export and retrieve a job run.
[agent:result] 
